ensive way to  generate all of your electricity also a  big problem is by growing all of these  crops in fields it takes up valuable  space which could be used to grow food  so those are the issues with biofuels  the next type of renewable energy source  we're looking at is wind power now you  probably have seen wind turbines in  fields before that look a little bit  like my drawing here so basically the  way that it operates is you have a  turbine which turns when the wind hits  the turbine it is then connected to this  part here which is a corded generator  which generates electricity now the  positive things about wind power just  like many of these types of renewable  energy is they release they don't  release any carbon dioxide so they don't  release any pollutants into the air also  once it's built it actually has cheap  running costs so it's not expensive once  the actual turbines are built in the  first place  now a few people complain that they  think they're ugly and noisy they can  also be quite expensive to build in the  first place now the big negative when it  comes to wind power is it must be windy  in order for them to work so on a really  non windy day they're not going to be  generating much energy if any energy at  all the third one we're going to learn  about are called solar cells so solar  cells you've probably seen these on the  roofs of certain houses but essentially  the way that it works is you have light  so usually sunlight that hits these  solar cells and it converts that energy  directly to electricity  now the advantages of this is you can  have these solar cells in really remote  places where other types of electricity  generation would not be suitable they're  also once they're built they they are  extremely cheap to run so once they're  built it's essentially not having to  spend much money on the upkeep of them  at all now the massive negative about  solar cells is they do not work at night  time when there is no sunlight to hit  the cells obviously there'll be no  electricity being generated the other  big problem with them is they're really  expensive to make in the first place so  once they're built they're really cheap  to run but it's expensive to make them  initially the next type of renewable  energy is hydroelectric power  hydroelectric or hydroelectricity so the  way that hydroelectric power works is  this there's a what happens is there's a  river what people do is they build a dam  across the river and this dam causes  flooding of the area behind what then  happens is at a slightly different level  so like at a lower level the river  continues underneath so what happens for  hydroelectricity is water is let through  the dam and within the dam there were  lots of turbines a little bit like in  wind power but there are turbines in the  dam that turn when the water drops  through so essentially that's how  hydroelectric power works now the  positive side the positive side of it is  hydroelectric power is really good for  meeting energy or energy demand so for  example if a city suddenly needed lots  and lots more electricity they just let  more water pass through the dam turning  the turbines at a faster rate or a  greater rate producing more electricity  they're also extremely reliable  now the downside of them is first of all  it causes the destruction of habitats  that's where animals live so when we  initially build the dam and flood the  surroundings it kill it basically  destroys lots of animals habitats it's  also extremely expensive to build one of  these dams in the first place now the  final type of renewable energy we're  going to look at are tidal barrages now  a tidal barrage works like this here I  have a river meeting the sea so this bit  here when I'm shading in green this is  the land and this bit here is the sea so  where you have the river meeting the the  sea you have an estuary which is just  the name of the bit where the river  meets the sea and the tidal barrages  built over the estuary now the way that  it works is again we have turbines  within the dam that turn when the tide  comes in and when the tide goes out now  the positive side of it again is they're  very reliable because the tides are very  reliable we can rely upon the tides  unlike for example the wind like many of  these different types of energy they  release no co2 once they're built now  for negative points about tidal barrages  similar to hydroelectric is it can often  damage animals habitats destroy their  habitats that's where they live when  it's built in the first place the other  negative point about tidal barrages is  it really affect boat access up and down  the estuary so guys that is basically a  summary summary of five different types  of renewable energy their advantages and  the disadvantages thank you
 

